{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161467-a-build-your-own-monster-frankenstein-based-legacy-for-a-wild-west-steampunk-game","id":1161467,"title":"A \"Build Your Own Monster\" Frankenstein-based Legacy for a Wild West/Steampunk Game","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-22T04:00:02-08:00","id":1161467,"title":"A \"Build Your Own Monster\" Frankenstein-based Legacy for a Wild West/Steampunk Game","content":"I was lucky enough to find myself playing a Mage 2E game in a Storyteller's absolutely spectacular Wild West/Steampunk homebrew setting, and I'm working on creating a thematic Legacy based on the Frankenstein mythos. I'd love any comments, insights, criticisms, etc. that you might have, and thank you very much in advance for your time!<br /> <br /> In brief, the Collegium Ex Mortis is a heavily scientific Legacy whose Attainments involve capturing and &quot;infusing&quot; multiple spirits into a corpse during a long surgical procedure involving a lab and (of course) an assistant (preferably named Igor). The end result is an animated corpse whose capabilities are determined by the Influences of the spirits you shove into it. <br /> <br /> I'm really a huge fan of the theme (the idea of creating a monster whose nature is determined by various spirit Influences almost overwhelms the creative part of my brain), as is my Storyteller. As for balance, I'm optimistic that there are enough &quot;checks and balances&quot; in the nature of the Attainments to ensure the Storyteller has a lot of grip on the leash, so to speak. (E.g. the Storyteller has control over which spirits are available to me, and ultimately decides upon the specific effects of the Influences I choose to infuse into the corpse).<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12jy5c8PtavmhXLJywygLbjIhVl04_JpoYxa1mqewtdg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Here's a link to the nearly-finished Legacy</a>, but you may want to skim the following notes on the setting to fully understand the flavor and some of the terminology:<ul><li><b>When</b>: 1875. <b>Where</b>: Southwestern USA</li> <li><b>Background</b>: Ten years ago, &quot;The One We Don't Name&quot; (it was totally Cthulhu) rose out of the Atlantic ocean, destroyed Boston, and unleashed a new age of &quot;what the fuckery&quot; upon the world. This new age, among other things, saw the rise of the Craftsmen (i.e. mages)</li> <li><b>Mages &amp; Magic</b>: The power stat for Craftsmen is Competency, rather than Gnosis, and &quot;magic&quot; is usually referred to as the Craft.</li> <li><b>Watchtowers</b>: The Five Watchtowers were redesigned. My character Awakened at the Tower of Sariel, with Spirit and Forces as its Ruling Arcana</li> <li><b>Orders</b>: The Five Orders have been replaced with the Three Schools, each affiliated with one of the Three Firms. They all appeared rather mysteriously as part of the &quot;what the fuckery&quot; aftermath of The One We Don't Name's rampage (only the Victorian School and the Iron Court are related to the Legacy, but the others are so cool I figured I'd include them):<ol class=\"decimal\"><li><u>The Victorian School of Craft</u> &amp; <u>the Iron Court</u>: After destroying Boston, The One We Don't Name crossed the Atlantic and annihilated the United Kingdom. But before he got there, the whole city of London was mysteriously teleported to Southern California. There, Queen Victoria, her Iron Court, and the entire population of New London continued about their daily business while being very British about the whole &quot;we were teleported across the ocean&quot; thing (i.e. they don't really talk about it). Craftsmen from the Victorian School tend to be pompous aristocrats who don't practice magic (how absurd!), but <i>science</i>, and are quite stuffy about it when interacting with the other firms. (Think a more neighbourly Technocracy with an aristocratic steampunk vibe and vague links to the God-Machine.)<br /> I play a Craftsman of the Victorian School, loyal to the Iron Court and Queen Victoria, with knowledge of Spirit, Death and Forces. Those three Arcanum, combined with the scientific focus of the Victorian School and the steampunk setting just <i>begged </i>for some Frankenstein, I thought.</li> <li><u>The Eldritch School of Craft</u> &amp; <u>the Great Dreamer Trading Company</u>: The GDTC is probably the most powerful Firm. They were the ones who stopped He Who Will Not Be Named after the destruction of the UK, and since then they've managed to get their fingers into <i>everything.</i> The GDTC is run by mysterious figures who are never seen, going by names like &quot;the Gentleman&quot; and &quot;Management&quot;. Eldritch Craftsmen are all addicted to Overblood, whatever <i>that</i> is, and require frequent injections of it. (It's all very Lovecraftian; the GDTC probably stopped He Who Will Not Be Named by inviting him onto the Board of Directors.)</li> <li><u>The Infernal School of Craft</u> &amp; <u>the Brass Administration</u>: Hell saw an opportunity to join the fun, and opened gateways to Earth. And Hell, as it turns out, is really quite civil! Their demonic representatives are very friendly, impeccably courteous, and, while they do require their Craftsman to enter into soul contracts for power, Infernal Craftsman never seem to complain about their lot in life. The Brass Administration also runs the 666 Transcontinental, a luxurious passenger train whose shortcuts through Hell make for interesting scenery <i>and </i>shorter travel times (not to mention giving my Victorian Craftsman a headache trying to come up with a scientific explanation for it all...)!</li> </ol></li> <li><b>Glass</b>: Lastly, there's the appearance of Glass: a naturally-forming substance arising all over the world, but concentrated in the southwestern USA in an area called the Glasslands. Glass comes in various colors, each with their own properties, dangers and rarities. Blue Glass, mentioned in the First Attainment, has the ability to imprison spirits.</li> </ul><br /> (Anywho, I'm just a player and not privy to the secrets our Storyteller undoubtedly has in store. So, on the off-chance you recognize some literary inspiration in the above, don't spoil my fun by pointing it out, please and thank you!)<br /> <br /> Thanks again for taking the time to read all of this, and for any insights you can provide!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:tahoma\">Amateur dinosaur hunter and extreme weather enthusiast, whose interests include spoken mime, armchair parkour, conspicuous ninjutsu and Schr\u00f6edinger's pentameter\u2014of which this sentence may or may not be an example.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13110-eunomiac","name":"Eunomiac","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}